1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,043
The dashboard is green.

2
00:00:02,043 --> 00:00:05,106
More people are opening the page.

3
00:00:05,106 --> 00:00:12,255
The team celebrates, then support hears that members still cannot finish the first task.

4
00:00:12,255 --> 00:00:19,404
A rising activity line did not answer the question we were trying to solve.

5
00:00:19,404 --> 00:00:29,617
Chapter Twelve tells a version of this failure: a dashboard looked healthy while the product was missing the customer outcome.

6
00:00:29,617 --> 00:00:35,745
The point is to choose a measure that belongs to the decision.

7
00:00:35,745 --> 00:00:41,872
In the membership service example, the business may care about sustained participation.

8
00:00:41,872 --> 00:00:45,957
A member cares about getting started without confusion.

9
00:00:45,957 --> 00:00:53,106
The team can first observe whether a person identifies and begins the intended action.

10
00:00:53,106 --> 00:01:02,298
Those are different levels of the metrics ladder in the book: business value, customer value, and near-term traction.

11
00:01:02,298 --> 00:01:07,404
The lower step does not automatically cause the upper one.

12
00:01:07,404 --> 00:01:10,468
We need to test the connection.

13
00:01:10,468 --> 00:01:21,191
If page opens rise while people still ask staff what to do, the green chart is a warning, not a win.

14
00:01:21,191 --> 00:01:26,809
Write the customer behavior in a sentence before selecting a metric.

15
00:01:26,809 --> 00:01:36,511
For this lesson, it is: a new member understands the first task and starts it without translation from staff.

16
00:01:36,511 --> 00:01:41,617
Now ask what observation would support or challenge that sentence.

17
00:01:41,617 --> 00:01:46,213
A useful measure can be counted or described consistently.

18
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:53,362
It also needs a definition that another person can apply to the same event.

19
00:01:53,362 --> 00:02:00,000
We will put that definition into the learning plan before seeing any result.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,120
Define a successful start before the test begins.

21
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:19,056
For a moderated session, that might mean the participant can point to the intended action, explain its purpose in their own words, and begin without staff explaining the instruction.

22
00:02:19,056 --> 00:02:25,236
The numerator is the number of observed participants who meet that definition.

23
00:02:25,236 --> 00:02:31,931
The denominator is the number of eligible participants who reach the first-task moment.

24
00:02:31,931 --> 00:02:39,142
Record exclusions before the session, such as an interrupted test or a technical outage.

25
00:02:39,142 --> 00:02:46,867
A second observer should be able to check the same notes and understand the count.

26
00:02:46,867 --> 00:02:51,502
That measure is close to the behavior we seek.

27
00:02:51,502 --> 00:02:54,592
It still cannot tell us everything.

28
00:02:54,592 --> 00:03:00,773
Ask the participant where they hesitated and what they expected to happen.

29
00:03:00,773 --> 00:03:08,498
That qualitative note may show whether the copy, confirmation, or task itself is the problem.

30
00:03:08,498 --> 00:03:12,618
Chapter Twelve also warns about destructive metric pairs.

31
00:03:12,618 --> 00:03:21,373
We could maximize time spent on the first task while saying we want people to begin quickly.

32
00:03:21,373 --> 00:03:27,039
Longer time might mean careful engagement, or it might mean confusion.

33
00:03:27,039 --> 00:03:31,159
We need the context before celebrating either direction.

34
00:03:31,159 --> 00:03:45,579
Pair the successful-start measure with a guardrail: the person should not have to ask staff to rescue the flow or repeat an action because the state is unclear.

35
00:03:45,579 --> 00:03:50,730
A planned measurement cannot promise a conversion or retention lift.

36
00:03:50,730 --> 00:04:00,000
The definition is a proposed measurement instrument; the outcome remains unknown until a test is run and reviewed.

37
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,034
Chapter Thirteen gives the learning plan ten components.

38
00:04:04,034 --> 00:04:08,067
The first five establish what we are testing.

39
00:04:08,067 --> 00:04:11,092
One: business problem and customer outcome.

40
00:04:11,092 --> 00:04:19,664
Our example names the friction at the first task and the member's need to begin with confidence.

41
00:04:19,664 --> 00:04:25,210
We will not claim a business lift from this small exercise.

42
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:27,731
Two: target persona or cohort.

43
00:04:27,731 --> 00:04:35,294
Use the living persona card from lesson four: a new member at the first-task moment.

44
00:04:35,294 --> 00:04:42,857
Add the source and the uncertainty so the card stays honest about what we know.

45
00:04:42,857 --> 00:04:44,370
Three: hypothesis statement.

46
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:56,975
We believe a clearer first-task card, for a new member in that moment, will help them identify and begin the intended action without staff translation.

47
00:04:56,975 --> 00:04:58,487
Four: experiment scope.

48
00:04:58,487 --> 00:05:08,067
Specify the test setting, who is eligible, how long observation lasts, and which version of the card they see.

49
00:05:08,067 --> 00:05:12,605
Exclude the reminder system and the rest of onboarding.

50
00:05:12,605 --> 00:05:15,126
Five: metrics and success criteria.

51
00:05:15,126 --> 00:05:26,723
Use the successful-start definition we just wrote, the qualitative hesitation notes, and a threshold set by the actual study owner before data arrives.

52
00:05:26,723 --> 00:05:28,739
These fields belong together.

53
00:05:28,739 --> 00:05:33,782
If the cohort is vague, a clean metric can mislead.

54
00:05:33,782 --> 00:05:41,849
If the outcome is vague, a detailed experiment may only test whether a screen is clickable.

55
00:05:41,849 --> 00:05:47,395
On this worksheet, leave the actual threshold marked for owner decision.

56
00:05:47,395 --> 00:05:49,412
That blank is honest.

57
00:05:49,412 --> 00:06:00,000
The team must fill it before running a real test; it must not choose a convenient number after looking at results.

58
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,154
The next five components keep the test from ending at a chart.

59
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:08,718
Six: timing and review strategy.

60
00:06:08,718 --> 00:06:18,974
Decide when the team checks for setup problems, when it reads the observations, and when it makes the next decision.

61
00:06:18,974 --> 00:06:21,026
Seven: triggers and tripwires.

62
00:06:21,026 --> 00:06:31,282
State what pattern would support continuing, what would require revision, and what would make the team stop or investigate harm.

63
00:06:31,282 --> 00:06:40,000
For our example, repeated confusion or unclear failure states would pause expansion even if some people begin.

64
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,154
The exact numerical threshold is still an owner decision before the test.

65
00:06:46,154 --> 00:06:47,692
Eight: pivot options.

66
00:06:47,692 --> 00:06:53,846
If members understand the card but never return, investigate the reminder path.

67
00:06:53,846 --> 00:07:01,026
If they reach the card but cannot tell whether the action worked, test confirmation.

68
00:07:01,026 --> 00:07:04,103
Those are possible branches, not findings.

69
00:07:04,103 --> 00:07:06,154
Nine: future state path.

70
00:07:06,154 --> 00:07:17,436
If the small test supports the hypothesis and passes its guardrail, name the next bounded question rather than announcing a full rollout.

71
00:07:17,436 --> 00:07:23,077
Perhaps the team tests the same instruction with another eligible group.

72
00:07:23,077 --> 00:07:24,615
Ten: hypothesis backlog.

73
00:07:24,615 --> 00:07:34,872
Save the original belief, result, learning, owner, and date so the next team does not start from an empty page.

74
00:07:34,872 --> 00:07:37,436
That is the book's order.

75
00:07:37,436 --> 00:07:45,128
The plan carries both a decision and a memory of how the decision was made.

76
00:07:45,128 --> 00:07:49,231
A result can be positive, negative, or inconclusive.

77
00:07:49,231 --> 00:08:00,000
The point is that each route has a responsible next action already visible before the room gets attached to one answer.

78
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,234
Before there is any data, walk the plan through two possible results.

79
00:08:06,234 --> 00:08:14,545
In the first, participants understand the action and begin, but several say the confirmation is unclear.

80
00:08:14,545 --> 00:08:17,662
The successful-start measure may look promising.

81
00:08:17,662 --> 00:08:22,338
The guardrail tells us the work is not finished.

82
00:08:22,338 --> 00:08:29,610
We record the uncertainty and test feedback after the action before broadening the scope.

83
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:34,805
In the second, participants cannot explain why the task matters.

84
00:08:34,805 --> 00:08:37,922
That result challenges the instruction itself.

85
00:08:37,922 --> 00:08:42,597
The team revises the purpose statement and tests again.

86
00:08:42,597 --> 00:08:44,675
These are imagined branches.

87
00:08:44,675 --> 00:08:47,792
Both results are possibilities, not findings.

88
00:08:47,792 --> 00:08:51,948
Notice how the metric definition changes the conversation.

89
00:08:51,948 --> 00:08:56,623
“More opens” would let both situations hide behind activity.

90
00:08:56,623 --> 00:09:02,338
“Understands and begins without translation” brings the actual behavior into view.

91
00:09:02,338 --> 00:09:09,610
The qualitative note explains why a count moved and what it cannot tell us.

92
00:09:09,610 --> 00:09:18,961
If the sample is too small, unrepresentative, or interrupted, mark the outcome inconclusive and choose a better test.

93
00:09:18,961 --> 00:09:26,234
Chapter Twelve asks us to tie measures to hypotheses and prepare to be wrong.

94
00:09:26,234 --> 00:09:31,429
Chapter Thirteen gives us a place to record that preparation.

95
00:09:31,429 --> 00:09:39,740
The worksheet is useful only if a team can follow it when the answer is inconvenient.

96
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:45,455
Keep the threshold, exclusions, and branch choices dated before the test.

97
00:09:45,455 --> 00:09:52,208
Then, after a real review, record what happened and what the team decided.

98
00:09:52,208 --> 00:10:00,000
Do not rewrite the original bet to make the result look inevitable afterward to anyone.

99
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,195
Pause and complete all ten fields for one small test.

100
00:10:05,195 --> 00:10:15,065
Start with the business problem and customer outcome, then the target cohort, hypothesis, scope, and metrics with success criteria.

101
00:10:15,065 --> 00:10:27,532
Add timing and review strategy, triggers and tripwires, pivot options, future state path, and the hypothesis backlog entry you will fill after the review.

102
00:10:27,532 --> 00:10:37,922
Define one measure precisely: who counts, what event counts, the observation window, exclusions, and who will inspect the raw evidence.

103
00:10:37,922 --> 00:10:45,714
Put a guardrail beside it so a rising number cannot hide a worse customer experience.

104
00:10:45,714 --> 00:10:52,468
Resume when the stop or pivot condition is written before any result appears.

105
00:10:52,468 --> 00:11:03,377
If this is your project, use information you have permission to share and keep participant notes in your team’s research system.

106
00:11:03,377 --> 00:11:07,013
Our example stays with the membership service.

107
00:11:07,013 --> 00:11:10,649
The book's framework supplies its ten fields.

108
00:11:10,649 --> 00:11:17,922
The loop at the end helps you follow the plan into the next decision.

109
00:11:17,922 --> 00:11:21,039
Look back at the decision file.

110
00:11:21,039 --> 00:11:34,545
You have a named problem, a source ledger, a living persona, a roadmap bet, a traceable handoff, two learning checkpoints, and a plan for reading results.

111
00:11:34,545 --> 00:11:38,182
The work is still open to correction.

112
00:11:38,182 --> 00:11:41,299
That is what makes it usable.

113
00:11:41,299 --> 00:11:50,130
In the next module, the question moves into a decision room with other people's risks and authority.

114
00:11:50,130 --> 00:11:56,364
Bring the evidence and the limits with you, clearly and honestly marked.

115
00:11:56,364 --> 00:12:00,000
Let the unanswered question remain visible there.
